 { As sometimes occurs, a sharp middlegame battle has evaporated into a roughly equal endgame. } 1. { -KEY- } c3 { Stabilizing the queenside. } ( { On the tempting } 1. c4 { Black could reply } Rd6 { preparing ...b7-b5 with some counterplay. } ) 
Rd6 2. Nf4 $1 { Kavalek establishes a barrier against the black king. } Bxb3 3. axb3 Rd7 { It is time to activate the rook. } ( { An alternative in this sense was } 3... Rd8 $5 4. Kxh2 Rg8 { heading for g4, when White's king is again marginalized from the battle. } ) 
4. Kxh2 Re7 5. b4 $1 { The first step in the plan of creating a passed pawn. } Re1 6. Kg3 Rb1 7. Nd3 Kg6 8. Nc5 $1 { White is happy to trade the b-pawns since it will accelerate the creation of a passed pawn on the c-file. } Rxb2 9. Nxb7 Ra2 ( { Black defends against } 9... Ra2 9. Nc5 { in advance; but the active } h5 { might have been better - Chekhov, and only if } 10. Nc5 { then 10...Ra2 } ) 
10. c4 $1 { The passed pawn moves forward. Objectively the position is still balanced, but in practice White's moves are easier to find. } Ra3+ $2 { A serious mistake, helping White's king to approach the passed pawn. } ( { a) } 10... Rc2 $6 { is equally unconvincing in view of } 11. Na5 h5 12. b5 $1 ) 
 ( { b) But } 10... Ra4 $1 { would have created sufficient interference for a draw; e.g. } 11. Bc5 ( { after } 10... Ra3+ 10. Nd6 $6 Rxb4 12. Kf4 h5 13. Ke5 h4 14. c5 h3 { followed by ...Rb1-c1, White is even on the brink of defeat; } ) 
h5 12. b5 axb5 13. cxb5 h4 14. Kh3 Kh5 15. Nd6 Ra5 $1 16. Be3 ( { or } 15... Ra3+ 15. Bb4 Ra4 { and there is nothing better than } 17. Bc5 { repeating moves; } ) 
Ra3 17. Nxf5 Ra5 $1 { and White can't make progress, e. g. } 18. Nd4 Ra3 { etc. } ) 
11. Kf4 Rb3 $6 { A bad place for the rook, as we will see next. } ( { a) } 11... h5 { is equally unsatisfactory due to } 12. c5 Rb3 ( { or } 12... h4 13. c6 h3 14. c7 h2 15. c8=Q $11 h1=Q $11 16. Qg8 ) 
13. c6 Rxb4 14. Ke5 Rc4 15. Nc5 { - Chekhov. } ) 
 ( { b) But } 11... Ra4 { similar to the 10...Ra4 in the previous note albeit a tempo down, was quite feasible; e.g. } 12. Ke5 Rxb4 13. Nd6 f4 $1 14. Kxf4 h5 { with drawing chances. Compared with the position after 10...Ra4 11 Nd6 Rxb4 12 Kf4 h5, here the f-pawn is no longer on the board, but that might not be too significant. } ) 
12. b5 $1 { A strong breakthrough, possibly overlooked by Olafsson. } axb5 13. c5 Rb4 14. Ke5 { It transpires that all White's pieces are excellently placed to assist the passed pawn. } Rc4 ( { Or } 14... f4 15. c6 { and the white passed pawn is the stronger one - Byrne. } ) 
15. Nd6 Rc1 16. Nxb5 ( { The right capture; after } 16. Nxf5 $6 b4 { Black would achieve counterplay. } ) 
Kf7 $6 { In an already difficult position, this might be the losing move. } ( { a) } 16... h5 { was strongly met by the 'curtain' } 17. Nc3 ) 
 ( { b) But by } 16... f4 $5 17. Kxf4 h5 { Black could have sold a pawn for a tempo, in similar fashion to the 11...Ra4 subline above. Here Black has lost the a6-pawn as well, but that might not be too important either. One idea is that } 18. Nc3 Kf7 19. Ke5 h4 20. c6 h3 21. c7 Re1 22. Ne4 { can be met by } Rxe4 $1 23. Kxe4 h2 { queening with check. } ) 
17. Kd6 $1 { In pawn ending style, the white king assists the c-pawn while obstructing its black counterpart. } ( { In contrast, } 17. Nd6 $6 Ke7 18. Nxf5 Kd7 19. Kd5 ( { the materialistic } 19. Nxh6 $2 { fails to } Kc6 { and 20...Rxc5+ } ) 
h5 { yields Black chances for a draw. } ) 
f4 { Now that the c-pawn has become unstoppable, there is nothing better. } 18. c6 f3 19. c7 h5 20. Nc3 $1 { A well-calculated final blow. } Rxc3 21. Bxc3 f2 22. c8=Q f1=Q ( { Black resigned, not waiting for } 22... f1=Q $11 23. Qe6 Kf8 24. Qe7 Kg8 25. Qg7 { - Byrne. } ) 

 1-0

