 { This position should be about equal, but Georgiev managed to squeeze a full point by skilfully co-ordinating his minor pieces. } 1. { -KEY- } f3 $1 { A small, but important move that prevents ...g5-g4, fixing White's kingside pawns on dark squares. Interestingly, in endgames with such a material relation it often makes sense for the side fighting against the rook to put his pawns on squares of the same colour as his bishop, in order to facilitate their protection and create strongholds on squares of that colour, such as e4 later on. } Ke6 ( { Black could have considered the active } 1... c4 { as well; e.g. } 2. Na5 c3 3. Kc2 Ra1 4. Nb3 Rxa4 5. Kxc3 Ke5 { and a draw seems like the most likely result. } ) 
2. Na5 { Heading for the protected square at c4. } Ke5 ( { Obviously, the straightforward } 2... Kd5 $6 3. Bb3 Kd4 $4 { failed to } 4. Nc6 ) 
3. Nc4+ Kd4 $6 ( { Georgiev prefers } 3... Ke6 { , probably because the king will soon have to retreat anyway. White could reply } 4. Ne3 f4 5. gxf4 gxf4 6. Nc2 { in similar style to the game. } ) 
4. Ne3 f4 5. gxf4 gxf4 6. Nc2+ { The white forces seem to have been pushed back, but this is temporary. } Kd5 ( { Here, the desirable } 6... Kc4 $4 { obviously fails to } 7. Na3 { so the king must move backwards. } ) 
7. Be2 $1 { Eyeing the a6-pawn while creating a barrier against the black king. } ( { Besides, } 7. Be2 { gets ready to meet } c4 $2 { by } 8. Bxc4 ) 
Rb6 $2 { Very passive. The rook is famous for being a great attacker - and a lousy defender. Moreover, under this kind of material relation the side with the rook is required to play actively, exploiting the great mobility of this piece (compare Grigorian-Tal and Dutreeuw-Hellsten, Examples 229 and 230 in the previous chapter), as otherwise the minor pieces will soon dominate the board. } ( { a) Perhaps Mitkov discarded } 7... Rb3 8. Bxa6 { in view of } Rxf3 $2 ( { but } 8... c4 $1 { instead would have yielded him sufficient counterplay; e.g. } 9. Bc8 Kc5 10. Ke2 Rb2 11. Bf5 Ra2 ) 
9. Bb7 ) 
 ( { b) Even } 7... a5 { was better than the text, as the rook stays in enemy territory. } ) 
8. Kc3 a5 ( { If } 8... Kc6 { White should avoid the tempting } 9. a5 Rb8 10. Bxa6 $2 Ra8 { and Black is helped by a lesser number of pawns on the board. } ) 
 ( { Instead, the calm } 8... a5 8. Bd3 { heading for e4 just like in the game, keeps excellent winning chances. } ) 
9. Bd3 $1 { Georgiev prepares Be4, after which his bishop and f3-pawn will mutually defend themselves. } ( { At the same time, he prevents } 9. Bd3 Rb1 $2 { on account of } 10. Ne3 ) 
Rb8 10. Be4+ Ke6 11. Na3 $1 { The knight returns to its preferred square on c4. By now it is clear that Black will have a hard time defending his pawns, while White's pawns, conveniently located on light squares, are much more difficult to get at. } ( { Less constructive was } 11. Kc4 $6 Kd6 ) 
Rb4 12. Bc2 Rb8 13. Nc4 Ra8 14. Be4 Ra7 15. Nd2 $1 { Finally, White gets ready to use his king in the battle, exploiting the fact that the enemy rook is temporarily out of play. } Kd6 16. Kc4 Rc7 17. Nb3 { Black's defensive resources are exhausted, and the rest is easy. } Ra7 18. Kb5 ( { Sidestepping a final trap: } 18. Nxc5 $4 Rc7 ) 
Re7 19. Nxa5 Re8 20. Nc4+ 
 1-0

